  "query_preview": "..The large scale pattern has a trough to our west \nacross the western Great Basin and California, an upper low tracking \nnortheast through western Utah and a ridge of high pressure centered \nover northeast Mexico and Texas. The flow across southeast Arizona \nbetween the trough to the west and the upper high to the east \nremains southerly into Thursday. PWAT's are currently in the 0.85 to \n1.0 inch range across much of the forecast area, with lesser amounts \naround 0.65 inches over far western Pima County/the White Mountains \nof northern Greenlee County and higher values near 1.15 inches over \nfar southeastern Cochise County. \n\nWith the southerly flow, PWAT's are expected to climb into the 1.1 \nto 1.3 range late today and persist through Wednesday. PWATs will \nlower slightly back down to around 1.0 inch on Thursday. Both the \nGFS/ECMWF track a disturbance around the back side of the high from \nMexico northward across our eastern counties on Wednesday. There are \nsome differences in the track, with the GFS closer to the New Mexico \nstate line and the Euro further west. This feature will help to \nenhance shower/thunderstorm development Wednesday, especially under \nand to the east of this disturbance. The current forecast has a \nchance of showers and thunderstorms across the eastern two-thirds of \nthe forecast area today, spreading westward Wednesday and Thursday \nacross most of southeast Arizona. Even with this westward extension, \nthe highest POPs remain to the east of Tucson, over Cochise, Graham \nand Greenlee counties today through Thursday. WPC has much of \nsoutheast Arizona in the MRGL category through Wednesday and then \nthe eastern half of the forecast area in MRGL on Thursday, with the \nstronger thunderstorms over these days capable of producing heavy \nrainfall and an isolated threat of flash flooding.  \n\nDrier air then begins to overspread southeast Arizona from the west \non Friday as the flows turns southwest/west in response to a system \ntracking east across the northern tier of states. There will still \nbe a slight chance of showers and thunderstorms on Friday across far \neastern areas. However by Saturday, PWATs lower down to around 0.75 \ninches and convection should be shunted to our east across New \nMexico and southward into Sonora Mexico. \n\nIt appears this dry spell won't last long as the combination of an \nupper low moving into the central California coast and Hurricane \nPolo moving northward along the Mexico coast will help spread \nmoisture back across southeast Arizona from the south. This moisture \nbegins to move back into the area on Sunday for a slight chance of \nshowers and thunderstorms, with deeper moisture moving in Monday and \nTuesday and a chance of showers and thunderstorms across most of \nsoutheast Arizona. The track of Hurricane Polo will depend highly \nupon the timing and depth of the trough/upper low moving into \nCalifornia. At this point, there is still a lot of uncertainty on \nthe exact track of Polo once it reaches as far north as Cabo. \n\nOtherwise, high temperatures will be 2-5 degrees below normal \nthrough Thursday, warming to around to slightly above normal Friday \nthrough Sunday and then lowering back to a couple of degrees below \nnormal early next week.",
